// const CACHE_NAME  = 'workhive-shell-v349';  // bump 2026-09-10: A REGISTERED QUEUE IS NOT A WORKING QUEUE, and the connectivity widget could not tell them apart. whGetQueueDepth already marks an unreadable queue by writing `perSurface[name] = -1` instead of a count - which happens whenever indexedDB.open fails: private browsing, site data blocked, or a quota exceeded on a shared plant tablet. connectivity-widget.js counted the KEYS of that object, so a -1 read as a present, healthy queue and the help text promised "pending writes save to this device and drain automatically ... you can keep working offline" on a page whose queue could not accept a single write. That is the SAME false promise this widget was built to stop on 2026-08-04 - when it told a seller their work was safe while an offline Save fired into a dead network - arriving through a different door: not "this page never had a queue" but "this page has one and it is broken". The sentinel existed and nothing read it. Now only a queue that ANSWERED counts, and a single broken queue silences the promise for the whole page, because that is this file's own rule applied consistently: a page where one surface can queue and another cannot does not save your work, it saves some of it, and the person has no way to know which. Verified across all six states: no queue and a broken queue both give the honest "do not submit without a connection", one or two healthy queues still promise safety, and healthy-plus-broken now warns instead of promising. Found while chasing a related lead - a failed offline enqueue leaves a person with a dead button and no message, since utils.js's global unhandledrejection handler only LOGS - which is recorded in the roadmap with its uncertainty rather than acted on, because two structural passes over the 16 enqueue call sites disagreed with each other. This half needed no such analysis: it is provable from the code. Bumped because connectivity-widget.js is precached, and without it an in