// const CACHE_NAME  = 'workhive-shell-v348';  // bump 2026-09-10 (W3-JN, the offline-3g cluster's ROOT): ONE LOST REQUEST OUT OF 65 DESTROYED THE ENTIRE PWA INSTALL, AND NOTHING SAID SO. install ran `cache.addAll(SHELL_FILES)`, which is ATOMIC - any single failed entry rejects, the rejection escapes waitUntil, and the worker goes from `installing` straight to `redundant`. The person is then left with NO worker at all: no precache, no offline fallback, no network-first copy of the page they were on, and no message anywhere. Measured in a browser rather than argued: registering under the journey prover's own offline-3g emulation returned `registered:true, state:installing, finalState:REDUNDANT` with sw.js itself never among the dropped requests, while the same page with routing present but nothing dropped installed and activated normally - so the LOSS killed it, not the instrumentation. The arithmetic is the argument and it is not only about a stress test: the install survives only if all 65 entries land, so at offline-3g's 1-in-12 that is (11/12)^65 = 0.35%, at an ordinary 1% request loss it is (0.99)^65 = 52% - HALF of real plant-floor installs failing completely - and at 3% it is 14%. Each file now gets ONE retry (a dropped request is usually unlucky rather than unavailable, the same reasoning browser-floor.js applies to a dropped script) and a file that still will not come is left out rather than taking the install down with it; a worker holding 63 of 65 files is a working shell, a worker that never activates is nothing, and the fetch handler already falls back to the network and then the offline shell for anything absent. Verified both ways after the change: WITH drops -> activated, 65/65 cached (the retry recovered every one); without drops -> activated, 65/65. THIS IS ALSO WHY THE OFFLINE-3G CLUSTER LOOKED THE WAY IT DID, and the correction is owed to every claim made about it: under that condition the service worker NEVER installed, so every offline-3g journey has been measuring a platform whose worker was inert - the precache, the _appNav navigation copy and the _appScript copy were all absent, and the v345 note's "a cold first visit whose navigation is the dropped request is not recoverable on the client at all" was reasoning about a worker that was not running. Those rows are being re-walked against a platform that now actually has one. AND ONE CONSEQUENCE OF THIS FIX, HANDLED IN THE SAME BUMP because it is caused by it: a PARTIAL shell is now possible for the first time, so the offline fallback itself may be the file that was left out - and the old `new Response('', {status: 503})` would then paint a person a WHITE PAGE, which tells them less than the browser's own "no internet" screen. Both fallback sites now return Response.error() when the shell is not cached, handing the navigation back to the browser instead of inventing a blank one. Before this turn that branch was unreachable - the atomic install meant the shell was either wholly cached or the worker never activated at all.
